You are helping build an evaluation set for a chemical safety Q&A assistant.
The assistant answers questions from warehouse and lab workers using only the
Safety Data Sheets (SDS) of the chemicals listed below. Each SDS follows the
standard 16-section format (Identification, Hazards, Composition, First aid,
Fire-fighting, Accidental release, Handling and storage, Exposure controls/PPE,
Physical and chemical properties, Stability and reactivity, Toxicology,
Ecology, Disposal, Transport, Regulatory, Other).

Chemicals in the corpus (name — hazard profile):
<PASTE chemical_name — hazard_profile LINES FROM sources.csv HERE>

Write 70 candidate questions a real worker might ask. Requirements:

1. Category quotas (approximate):
   - physical_property (flash point, boiling point, explosion limits, pH,
     solubility, physical state): 11
   - ppe (gloves, eye/face protection, respiratory protection): 10
   - exposure_limit (OSHA PEL, ACGIH TLV, NIOSH IDLH): 7
   - compatibility (can X be stored near Y; what must X be kept away from): 11
   - first_aid (eye/skin contact, inhalation, ingestion): 7
   - fire (extinguishing media, what not to use, combustion products): 6
   - hazard_id (signal word, hazard classes, carcinogenicity): 6
   - unanswerable: 12 (see rule 5)

2. Spread questions across all chemicals; no chemical in more than 4 questions.

3. Write the way a busy worker talks: short, informal, sometimes imprecise
   ("what gloves for caustic soda?", "is it ok to keep acetone by the bleach?").
   Use common names and synonyms sometimes (caustic soda, bleach, wood alcohol,
   glacial acetic acid). Vary phrasing; do not start every question the same way.

4. Compatibility questions must pair two chemicals or a chemical with a common
   substance class. Include these exact safety-critical ones:
   - "Can I store bleach next to an acid?"
   - "Can sodium azide be kept near hydrochloric acid?"

5. Unanswerable questions must be things an SDS genuinely does not contain, e.g.:
   price or supplier stock, shelf life after opening, a chemical NOT in the list,
   medical dosing or treatment for a specific person, legal/shipping permission
   in a specific country, comparisons requiring outside data ("which is the
   safest solvent overall?"), company-specific procedures.
   Do NOT make "unanswerable" questions that are merely about missing values in
   an SDS (like a flash point for a solid) — those count as answerable.

6. Do NOT include answers. Do NOT invent facts about the chemicals.

Output format: JSON Lines only, no commentary, one object per line:
{"question": "...", "chemical": "...", "category": "..."}
For unanswerable questions about chemicals outside the list, set "chemical" to
the name used in the question.